.TH MAILFIRE 1 "October 17, 2026" "mailfire" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
mailfire \- send email from the command line via Mailgun, Gmail, or SMTP
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B mailfire
.RB [ send ]
.RI [ flags ]
.br
.B mailfire
.RB [ send ]
.BI \-\-preview " path"
.RI [ flags ]
.br
.B mailfire validate
.RB [ \-\-json ]
.RB [ \-c
.IR path ]
.br
.B mailfire resend
.RB [ \-\-list " | " \-\-force " | " \fIid\fR...]\&
.RB [ \-\-json ]
.RB [ \-c
.IR path ]
.br
.B mailfire auth
.BR login " | " status " | " refresh " | " logout
.B \-\-provider gmail
.RI [ flags ]
.br
.B mailfire queue
.BR list " | " stats
.RB [ \-\-json ]
.RB [ \-c
.IR path ]
.br
.B mailfire queue show
.RB [ \-\-raw ]
.RB [ \-\-json ]
.I id
.RB [ \-c
.IR path ]
.br
.B mailfire queue delete
.IR id ...
.RB [ \-\-json ]
.RB [ \-c
.IR path ]
.br
.B mailfire version
.RB [ \-\-json ]
.br
.B mailfire export\-templates
.RB [ \-\-json ]
.I folder
.br
.B mailfire
.BR \-h " | " \-\-help
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B mailfire
sends email from the command line through one of three providers,
selected with
.BR [sending].provider :
the Mailgun HTTP API (the default), the Gmail API with send-only OAuth
authorization, or standard SMTP submission to an existing email provider or
local relay.
The message body is read from piped standard input, a regular file with
.BR \-\-file ,
or an explicit string with
.BR \-\-message .
Plain text, Markdown, and HTML bodies are supported; Markdown and HTML
are rendered into a built-in, responsive email template or a custom template file.
.PP
Without a subcommand,
.B send
is assumed.
On success the message ID is printed to standard output: Mailgun's or
Gmail's ID, or for SMTP the Message-ID header Mailfire generated.
For SMTP, success means the submission server accepted the message (a 250
reply), not that it reached the recipient's mailbox.
.PP
If sending fails because of a network error, a temporary server error, or rate
limiting, the composed message is stored for later retrying with
.B mailfire resend
(unless deferral is disabled).
.SH SUBCOMMANDS
.TP
.B send
Compose and send an email.
This is the default subcommand.
.TP
.B validate
Check, step by step, that the configuration file is found and valid TOML
(or that
.B MAILFIRE_*
environment variables replace it),
that all mandatory keys are well-formed, and that the selected provider
accepts the credentials: Mailgun checks the API key and domain; SMTP connects,
negotiates TLS, and authenticates without issuing MAIL, RCPT, or DATA (no
recipient is probed); Gmail checks the stored authorization, refreshing it
when it is about to expire.
No mail is sent.
.TP
.B auth
Manage the Gmail authorization; see
.BR "GMAIL AUTHORIZATION" .
.TP
.B resend
Retry deferred messages, oldest first.
A message is removed only after successful delivery.
Messages that are not yet eligible for a retry are skipped unless
.B \-\-force
is given; see
.BR "DEFERRED MESSAGES" .
.TP
.B queue
Inspect and delete deferred messages without sending.
.B queue list
shows each message with its attempt count and next retry;
.BI "queue show " id
shows its recipients, subject, age, attempts, last error, and next retry;
.B queue stats
shows totals;
.BI "queue delete " id ...
deletes the given messages.
.BR list ", " show ", and " stats
never send, change, or remove messages.
.TP
.B version
Print the version, commit, and build date.
.TP
.BI export\-templates " folder"
Export all four built-in templates as self-contained, editable HTML template files.
Creates the folder if needed and refuses to overwrite existing template files.
No configuration or provider credentials are required.
.PP
Every subcommand accepts
.BR \-h " and " \-\-help .
.SH OPTIONS
.SS Flags of send
.TP
.BI \-\-to " addr"
Recipient, repeatable.
Required, at least once, unless
.B \-\-preview
is given.
Accepts
.B user@example.com
or
.BR "Name <user@example.com>" .
Comma-separated lists are not supported,
because commas are legal in display names.
.TP
.BI \-\-cc " addr"
Carbon copy recipient, repeatable.
.TP
.BI \-\-bcc " addr"
Blind carbon copy recipient, repeatable.
.TP
.BI \-\-from " addr"
Sender; overrides the
.B from
setting of the configuration file.
Required if the configuration file does not set
.BR from .
.TP
.BI \-\-subject " text"
Subject line.
Required unless
.B \-\-preview
is given.
.TP
.B \-\-txt
Body is plain text.
This is the default.
.TP
.B \-\-md
Body is Markdown, rendered into the selected template.
A plain-text alternative is sent as well.
Local images referenced as
.B ![alt](path/to/image.png)
are attached and displayed inline; remote image URLs are left untouched.
.TP
.B \-\-html
Body is an HTML fragment, wrapped in the selected template.
A plain-text alternative is derived from it.
.TP
.B \-\-html\-raw
Body is a complete HTML document, sent as-is.
A plain-text alternative is derived from it.
.PP
Exactly one of the four body format flags may be given.
.TP
.BI \-\-file " path"
Read the body from a file instead of standard input.
Only regular files are accepted; named pipes and devices are refused.
.TP
.BI \-\-message " text"
Use an explicit body string instead of standard input. Aliases:
.BR \-\-m " and " \-m .
Cannot be combined with \-\-file, even when either value is empty.
Terminal standard input is never read. An empty body is permitted only
with attachments; otherwise the error is
"mail body missing: use stdin, --file or --message".
Piped input is still read when attachments are supplied.
.PP
Sender and recipient mailboxes require dotted DNS domains with valid,
nonempty labels and no leading or trailing hyphens. Quoted names and local
parts, plus addresses and Unicode are supported. No DNS lookup is performed.
Invalid input is rejected before sending and is never deferred.
.TP
.BI \-\-template " name"
Email template:
.BR card " (default), " card\-serif ", " plain " or " plain\-serif .
Overrides
.B template
or
.B template_file
in the
.B [sending]
section of the configuration file.
Only with
.B \-\-md
or
.BR \-\-html .
.PP
Cannot be combined with
.BR \-\-template\-file .
.TP
.BI \-\-template\-file " path"
Use a custom Go text/template file, overriding the configured template.
Only with
.B \-\-md
or
.BR \-\-html .
Cannot be combined with
.BR \-\-template ,
even when either value is empty.
Paths support a leading ~; relative paths use the current working directory.
Available fields are .Subject, .Lang (default en), .Preheader, .Body and .Footer.
Escape plain text with {{html .Subject}} or {{html .Preheader}};
insert HTML with {{.Body}} and {{.Footer}}.
CSS is inlined and Outlook conditional comments are preserved.
.TP
.BI \-\-preheader " text"
Hidden inbox preview text.
Only with
.B \-\-md
or
.BR \-\-html .
.TP
.BI \-\-footer " path"
HTML fragment appended as footer.
Only with
.B \-\-md
or
.BR \-\-html .
.TP
.BI \-\-attach " path"
Attach a file, repeatable.
.TP
.B \-\-test
Use Mailgun test mode: the API validates the request, but nothing is delivered.
Test sends are never deferred.
SMTP and Gmail have no validation-only mode, so
.B \-\-test
is a usage error with them rather than a real delivery; use
.B validate
or
.B \-\-preview
instead.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-defer
Fail immediately instead of storing the message for a later resend.
.TP
.BI \-\-timeout " duration"
Per-request timeout of the selected provider: for Mailgun and Gmail the
API request (connection setup, upload, response; for Gmail also a token
refresh), for SMTP the whole connection and transaction (connect, TLS,
authentication, submission).
Accepts Go duration syntax such as
.BR 15s " or " 1m .
Overrides the provider's
.B timeout
setting
.RB ( [mailgun] ", " [smtp] ", or " [gmail] );
the default is 30 seconds.
.TP
.BI \-\-dedup\-key " key"
Skip repeats of this notification: a send with the same key, sending
domain, and recipients is skipped (exit status 0) within the cooldown
after a delivery, while an earlier one is deferred, or for one minute
after an interrupted attempt.
See
.BR "NOTIFICATION DEDUPLICATION" .
.TP
.BI \-\-cooldown " duration"
Suppression window started by this send when it is delivered, such as
.BR 30m .
Requires
.BR \-\-dedup\-key .
Overrides
.BR [dedup].cooldown ;
the default is one hour.
.TP
.BI \-\-preview " path"
Render the body locally and write the HTML to
.I path
instead of sending; with
.BR \- ,
write only the HTML to standard output.
See
.BR "LOCAL PREVIEW" .
.TP
.B \-\-json
Print the result as one JSON document on standard output; see
.BR "JSON OUTPUT" .
Cannot be combined with
.BR \-\-preview .
.TP
.BI \-c ", " \-\-config " path"
Use this configuration file instead of searching the default locations.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Show help.
.SS Flags of resend
.TP
.B \-\-list
Show deferred messages (ID, date, to, subject, size) without sending.
.TP
.B \-\-force
Ignore the cooldown and backoff.
With
.I id
arguments, also retry messages that were rejected permanently.
.TP
.BI \-\-timeout " duration"
Per-request timeout for each resent message, as for
.BR send ;
it overrides the timeout of the provider each message is resent through.
A run retrying several messages may therefore take longer than a single
timeout.
.TP
.B \-\-json
Print the result, or with
.B \-\-list
the listing, as one JSON document; see
.BR "JSON OUTPUT" .
.TP
.BI \-c ", " \-\-config " path"
Use this configuration file.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Show help.
.PP
Without further arguments all eligible deferred messages are retried;
with one or more
.I id
arguments only those messages are retried.
.B \-\-list
cannot be combined with IDs.
.SS Flags of queue
.TP
.B \-\-raw
For
.BR show :
print the stored request (message content, attachments included) instead of
the metadata. It never contains credentials.
.TP
.B \-\-json
Print the result as one JSON document; see
.BR "JSON OUTPUT" .
With
.BR "show \-\-raw" ,
the stored request is included base64-encoded.
.TP
.BI \-c ", " \-\-config " path"
Use this configuration file.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Show help.
.PP
.B queue delete
validates all IDs first: if any is unknown, it reports them and deletes
nothing.
.SS Flags of validate
.TP
.BI \-\-timeout " duration"
Timeout of the provider check; overrides the provider's
.B timeout
setting.
.TP
.B \-\-json
Print the result as one JSON document; see
.BR "JSON OUTPUT" .
.TP
.BI \-c ", " \-\-config " path"
Use this configuration file.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Show help.
.SS Flags of auth
.TP
.B \-\-provider gmail
The provider to authorize (required).
.TP
.B \-\-no\-browser
login: print the consent URL instead of opening a browser, plus an SSH
port-forwarding hint.
.TP
.BI \-\-port " port"
login: listen for the OAuth callback on this local port instead of a random
one, so it can be forwarded over SSH.
.TP
.B \-\-manual
login: read the callback URL from standard input instead of receiving it
(requires
.BR \-\-port ).
.TP
.BI \-\-timeout " duration"
Token request timeout; overrides
.BR [gmail].timeout .
.TP
.B \-\-json
status, refresh, logout: print the result as one JSON document.
.TP
.BI \-c ", " \-\-config " path"
Use this configuration file (only its
.B [gmail]
section is read).
.SS Flags of version and export-templates
.TP
.B \-\-json
Print the result as one JSON document; see
.BR "JSON OUTPUT" .
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Show help.
.SH CONFIGURATION
.B mailfire
reads a TOML configuration file.
The first existing file of the following locations is used:
.PP
Linux and macOS:
.RS
.nf
\&./.mailfire.conf
$HOME/.config/mailfire/mailfire.conf
/etc/mailfire/mailfire.conf
.fi
.RE
.PP
Windows:
.RS
.nf
\&.\e.mailfire.conf
%APPDATA%\emailfire\emailfire.conf
%PROGRAMDATA%\emailfire\emailfire.conf
.fi
.RE
.PP
The option
.B \-\-config
overrides the search.
Environment variables override individual settings and can replace the
file; see
.BR ENVIRONMENT .
Strings must be quoted.
On Linux and macOS a warning is printed when the file is accessible by group
or others; fix it with
.BR "chmod 600" .
.PP
A system-wide configuration is usually insecure, because anyone who can read
it can use the API key.
.SS [mailgun]
.TP
.B api_key
String, mandatory.
The Mailgun API key.
.TP
.B region
String, mandatory.
Either
.B us
or
.BR eu .
.TP
.B timeout
Duration string, optional, default
.BR 30s .
Per-request timeout for Mailgun API calls, covering connection setup,
upload, and response reading.
Empty, zero, negative, and malformed values are rejected.
Can be overridden with
.BR \-\-timeout .
A
.B resend
run retrying several messages may take longer than a single timeout.
.SS [sending]
.TP
.B provider
String, optional, default
.BR mailgun .
Either
.B mailgun
or
.BR smtp .
Existing configurations continue using Mailgun unless this is set to SMTP.
.TP
.B domain
String, mandatory.
The sending domain as configured at Mailgun, or the stable Mailfire sending
and deduplication domain for SMTP.
.TP
.B from
String, optional.
Default sender, either
.B user@example.com
or
.BR "My Name <user@example.com>" .
Can be overridden with
.BR \-\-from .
If not set,
.B \-\-from
is mandatory.
.TP
.B template
String, optional, default
.BR card .
Email template for
.B \-\-md
and
.BR \-\-html :
.BR card ", " card\-serif ", " plain " or " plain\-serif .
Can be overridden with
.B \-\-template
or
.BR \-\-template\-file .
Mutually exclusive with a nonempty
.BR template_file .
.TP
.B template_file
String, optional. Custom template path for Markdown and HTML bodies.
Mutually exclusive with a nonempty
.BR template .
Either template flag overrides this setting.
A leading ~ is expanded; relative paths use the current working directory.
.SS [smtp]
Used only when
.B [sending].provider
is
.BR smtp .
No Mailgun API key or domain registration is required for SMTP submission.
.TP
.B host
SMTP server hostname, mandatory for SMTP.
.TP
.B port
SMTP server port from 1 through 65535, mandatory for SMTP.
.TP
.B security
Mandatory for SMTP:
.B tls
for implicit TLS (normally port 465),
.B starttls
for required STARTTLS (normally port 587), or
.B none
only for an explicitly trusted local plaintext relay. Mailfire never falls
back to plaintext when STARTTLS is unavailable or fails.
.TP
.BR username ", " password
Optional SMTP username/password pair. Both must be set together. Passwords
are never stored in deferred messages, logs, diagnostics, or JSON output.
Credentials are refused for
.BR "security = none" .
Common PLAIN, LOGIN, and CRAM-MD5 mechanisms are selected only after TLS.
.TP
.B timeout
Optional positive duration. It covers connection setup, TLS, authentication,
and SMTP submission. It is overridden by
.BR \--timeout .
.PP
SMTP acceptance (a 250 response after DATA) confirms only that the submission
server accepted the transaction; it does not prove final recipient delivery.
If any recipient is rejected at RCPT TO, the transaction is aborted before
DATA, so nobody receives a partial send; the error names the recipient.
Account setup is provider-specific: Gmail and other large providers usually
require an app password (or OAuth, see
.BR [gmail] );
an ordinary account password is not a supported shortcut.
.SS [gmail]
Used by
.B [sending].provider
.B gmail
and by
.BR "mailfire auth" .
.TP
.B credentials
Optional path of the owner-only credential file;
default
.I $HOME/.config/mailfire/gmail-credentials.json
.RI ( %APPDATA%\emailfire\egmail\-credentials.json
on Windows).
.TP
.B timeout
Optional positive duration for Gmail API and token requests;
overridden by
.BR \-\-timeout .
.SS [logging]
.TP
.B logfile
String, optional.
Logging is disabled unless a logfile is given.
One JSON object per sending attempt is written per line.
.TP
.B loglevel
String, optional, default
.BR information .
.RS
.TP
.B error
Failed attempts only: time, message ID, recipients, status code.
.TP
.B information
Time, message ID, recipients, subject, status code.
.TP
.B debug
Time, message ID, sender, recipients, subject, message size, status code,
API response body.
.RE
.TP
.B max_size
String, optional, default
.BR 50M .
When exceeded, the oldest log lines are removed.
Accepts a number of bytes or a K, M, or G suffix.
.SS [defer]
.TP
.B defer
Boolean, optional, default
.BR true .
Store messages whose delivery failed for a later retry.
.TP
.B defer_dir
String, optional.
Directory for deferred messages; created on demand.
Defaults to
.I $HOME/.local/share/mailfire
(\fI%LOCALAPPDATA%\emailfire\fR on Windows).
.TP
.B max_size
String, optional, default
.BR 50M .
When exceeded, the oldest deferred messages are removed.
.TP
.B resend_cooldown
Duration string, optional, default
.BR 5m .
Wait after deferral before
.B mailfire resend
retries a message; also the base of the retry backoff.
.TP
.B max_age
Duration string, optional, no default.
When set (for example
.BR 168h ),
messages deferred longer ago are removed whenever a message is deferred
or
.B mailfire resend
runs, and each removal is reported.
.B resend
checks the age again right before each retry, so a message that exceeds
.B max_age
during a run is removed rather than sent.
Without it, messages are kept until sent or deleted, subject to
.BR max_size .
.SS [dedup]
.TP
.B state_dir
String, optional.
Directory for notification deduplication state; created on demand.
Independent of the
.B [defer]
settings: deduplication also works with
.BR "defer = false" .
Defaults to
.I $HOME/.local/share/mailfire/dedup
(\fI%LOCALAPPDATA%\emailfire\ededup\fR on Windows).
Inside
.B defer_dir
only its subdirectory
.I dedup
may be used; symbolic links are resolved for this check.
.TP
.B cooldown
Duration string, optional, default
.BR 1h .
How long a delivered notification suppresses repeats with the same
.BR \-\-dedup\-key .
Can be overridden with
.BR \-\-cooldown .
.SS Example
.nf
[mailgun]
  api_key = "<your-api-key>"
  region = "eu"

[sending]
  domain = "example.com"
  from = "My Name <user@example.com>"

[logging]
  logfile = "~/logs/mailfire.log"
  loglevel = "information"
  max_size = "100M"

[defer]
  defer = true
  max_size = "100M"
  resend_cooldown = "5m"
.fi
.SH DEFERRED MESSAGES
A deferred message becomes eligible for
.B mailfire resend
once
.B resend_cooldown
has passed.
Each failed retry doubles the wait, measured from the latest attempt and
capped at one hour; a cooldown longer than one hour is used unchanged.
The stored message is never changed; only its retry information
(attempts, last attempt, last error) is recorded.
Messages deferred by older versions have no retry information and are
treated as never retried.
.PP
A message that Mailgun rejects permanently is kept, marked failed, and
skipped by later runs.
Retry it with
.BI "mailfire resend \-\-force " id
or remove it with
.BI "mailfire queue delete " id .
.PP
Concurrent
.B mailfire
processes coordinate through the lock file
.I .lock
in the defer directory, so overlapping runs cannot resend or delete the
same message twice.
Exactly-once delivery is still not guaranteed: when a request times out
or the connection drops after Mailgun accepted the message, it stays
deferred and a later resend may deliver it again.
.PP
A deferred message records the provider and the non-secret account it was
sent through (the Mailgun domain; the SMTP username, host, port, and security
mode; the Google account ID) and is resent through that provider with its
current credentials, even when the default provider changed meanwhile.
SMTP messages also keep their envelope (Bcc recipients included) and their
Message-ID, which stays the same on every retry.
Credentials are never stored with deferred messages.
If the provider is no longer configured or a different account is configured,
the message is not attempted; it stays queued with unchanged retry state and is
resent normally once the configuration is restored.
.PP
Duplicates are possible with every provider: when the connection drops or
times out after the server accepted the message (for SMTP, a lost final reply
after DATA), the outcome is unknown, the message stays deferred, and a later
resend may deliver it again.
.SH GMAIL AUTHORIZATION
.B mailfire auth login \-\-provider gmail
opens Google's consent page (installed-app loopback flow with PKCE and state
validation) and stores the access token, its expiry, and the refresh token in
the credential file (mode 0600).
Only the
.B gmail.send
scope is requested, plus
.BR openid ,
which yields only an opaque account ID used to bind deferred messages to the
account; no mailbox or profile access is requested.
Access tokens are refreshed automatically before sends; sends and refreshes
never open a browser.
.PP
.B auth refresh
exchanges the refresh token even when the cached access token is still
valid, without sending mail or touching the queue.
Google may expire refresh tokens unused for six months; schedule a monthly
refresh for infrequent senders under the same user as the sends.
Refreshing cannot prevent revocation, password or policy changes, or the
seven-day limit of OAuth apps in testing mode.
.B auth status
shows whether credentials exist,
.B auth logout
removes them locally (it does not revoke Google's grant).
.PP
Gmail sends only as the authenticated account or its configured send-as
aliases and enforces its own sending and size limits.
.SH LOCAL PREVIEW
.BI "send \-\-preview " path
renders the message body through the same pipeline as a send (Markdown,
template, preheader, footer, and CSS inlining) and writes the result to
.IR path ,
or to standard output for
.BR \- .
Unlike
.BR \-\-test ,
which asks Mailgun to validate a complete request, a preview never contacts
Mailgun and needs no configuration file, Mailgun settings, recipients,
sender, or subject.
Only the template selection is read from a configuration file,
.BR MAILFIRE_SENDING_TEMPLATE ,
or
.BR MAILFIRE_SENDING_TEMPLATE_FILE ;
other settings and variables are ignored.
Supplied addresses and subjects are still checked.
Nothing is sent, deferred, deduplicated, or logged.
.IP \(bu 2
.BR \-\-md " and " \-\-html
produce the rendered HTML part.
Local Markdown images are embedded as
.B data:
URLs, so the preview is one portable file; remote image URLs are unchanged.
A send attaches local images instead.
.IP \(bu 2
.B \-\-txt
shows the escaped body in a
.B <pre>
block.
.IP \(bu 2
.B \-\-html\-raw
writes the supplied document unchanged.
.PP
An existing output file is replaced atomically, keeping its permissions,
but only after rendering succeeded; a failed preview leaves it untouched.
A symbolic link as output is followed; a link to a missing file is
refused and left unchanged.
An output that is one of the inputs (body file or standard input
redirected from a file, footer, template, Markdown image, or
configuration file), also through a symbolic or hard link, is refused.
With
.BR "\-\-preview \-" ,
diagnostics go to standard error; use
.B ./\-
for a file named
.BR \- .
.PP
.BR \-\-attach ", " \-\-test ", " \-\-no\-defer ", " \-\-timeout ", " \-\-dedup\-key ", and " \-\-cooldown
only affect sending and are rejected with
.BR \-\-preview .
.B \-\-json
is rejected too: a preview writes HTML, not a command result.
A failed preview exits with status 1, or with 2 for an invalid
configuration file or template setting.
.SH JSON OUTPUT
With
.BR \-\-json ,
.BR send ,
.B resend
(also with
.BR \-\-list ),
.BR queue ,
.BR validate ,
.BR version ,
and
.B export\-templates
print exactly one JSON document on standard output, on one line, instead of
the human-readable output; also for failures, including invalid
invocations, whenever the command line contains a
.B \-\-json
flag.
.B \-\-json
counts only where it is read as a flag: not as the value of another flag
and not after a
.B \-\-
terminator.
Every document has
.BR schema_version " (currently 1), " command ", " outcome ", and " exit_code ;
when the exit status is not 0, an
.B error
object adds a stable
.B code
(such as
.BR usage_error ", " config_error ", " auth_failed ", " temporary_failure ", or " rejected )
and a human-readable
.BR message .
Timestamps are RFC 3339 in UTC.
Exit statuses are unchanged.
Diagnostics and warnings stay on standard error, and
.B \-\-help
always prints text.
Documents never contain the API key or message content, except the stored
request with
.BR "queue show \-\-raw \-\-json" .
.PP
.B resend
reports one record per message (sent, skipped, failed, or expired) and a
summary; its outcome is
.B partial
when some messages were sent and others failed.
The full schema, error codes, and compatibility rules are documented at
.UR https://mailfire.system42.io/reference/json-output/
.UE .
.SH NOTIFICATION DEDUPLICATION
Deduplication is opt-in: only sends with
.B \-\-dedup\-key
are checked.
A notification is identified by the key, the sending domain, and all
recipients (to, cc, and bcc, compared case-insensitively in any order);
different keys, domains, or recipients never interfere.
A send is skipped, with exit status 0 and a line starting with
.B skipped:
on standard output that says when it becomes eligible again, while
.IP \(bu 2
the same notification was delivered within its cooldown (the
.B \-\-cooldown
or
.B cooldown
of the delivered send; skipped repeats do not extend it),
.IP \(bu 2
the same notification is still deferred, even if marked permanently
rejected (retry it with
.B "resend \-\-force"
or remove it with
.BR "queue delete" ):
the first deferred message is kept and no duplicate is queued, or
.IP \(bu 2
an attempt was interrupted (for example by a crash) less than one minute
ago and may have been delivered.
.PP
Only a delivery starts the cooldown, by
.B send
or by a successful
.BR resend .
Failed and deferred sends do not, and
.B \-\-test
sends neither check nor start it.
If the system clock moves backward, affected cooldowns end immediately.
A concurrent send of the same notification waits until the first one's
outcome is recorded, so the two cannot both slip through.
Corrupt or unreadable state is an error and nothing is sent:
.B send
exits with status 2.
.B resend
keeps a deferred notification whose state it cannot use (unsafe
.BR state_dir ,
corrupt or unreadable state, a lock failure) queued without sending it,
reports it as failed, and exits with status 5.
Remove the named file to reset that notification.
.PP
To send a repeat anyway, omit
.BR \-\-dedup\-key .
.B "resend \-\-force"
is unrelated: it ignores the retry wait of deferred messages, not the
notification cooldown.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
Every configuration setting can be supplied through an environment
variable named
.BI MAILFIRE_ SECTION _ KEY\fR,
using the uppercased TOML names.
Precedence, highest first: command-line options, environment variables,
the configuration file, defaults.
A setting supplied by a command-line option ignores its environment and
file values without validating them.
Exception: setting both
.B MAILFIRE_SENDING_TEMPLATE
and
.B MAILFIRE_SENDING_TEMPLATE_FILE
to nonempty values is always a configuration error, even with
.B \-\-template
or
.BR \-\-template\-file .
Values use the same syntax and validation as in the configuration file.
Empty or whitespace-only variables are treated as unset and never clear a
file setting or default.
.PP
When no configuration file is found and at least one variable is set,
the environment is the only source; it must then supply the selected
provider's required settings (Mailgun: API key, region, and domain; SMTP:
host, port, security, and domain; Gmail: nothing beyond the provider).
A file given with
.B \-\-config
must still exist and be valid.
.TP
.B MAILFIRE_MAILGUN_API_KEY
.TQ
.B MAILFIRE_MAILGUN_REGION
.TQ
.B MAILFIRE_MAILGUN_TIMEOUT
The
.B [mailgun]
settings.
