.PP
Errors name the variable that supplied an invalid value.
.B mailfire validate
reports where settings came from; credentials are never shown.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success (sent, validated, resent, or information shown).
.TP
.B 1
Usage error: missing flags, unknown subcommand, conflicting formats.
.TP
.B 2
Configuration error: missing file, invalid TOML, missing or invalid keys.
.TP
.B 3
Authentication error: credentials were rejected (Mailgun 401 or 403, SMTP
535), or Gmail is not authorized (not logged in, or the authorization was
revoked or expired).
.TP
.B 4
Sending failed and the message was deferred for a later
.BR "mailfire resend" .
.TP
.B 5
Sending failed and the message was not deferred
.RB ( \-\-no\-defer
or
.BR "defer = false" ).
.PP
Exit status 5 is also used when the provider rejects a message permanently
(any other Mailgun or Gmail 4xx response, an SMTP 5xx reply such as a
rejected recipient, missing STARTTLS, or an untrusted certificate), when a
.B \-\-test
send fails with a retryable error (test sends are never deferred),
and when the message cannot be stored in the defer directory.
.B validate
exits with 4 when the provider cannot be reached reliably (the check can be
retried later), and with 2 when Mailgun does not know the domain.
.B auth
exits with 3 when Gmail is not authorized and 4 for a transient token
endpoint failure.
.B resend
returns the most severe outcome of all attempts, ranked 3, 5, 4, 0;
a message that fails with 4 stays deferred.
.B queue
exits with 1 for an unknown message ID and with 5 when the defer directory
cannot be read or a message cannot be deleted.
.B send
exits with 0 when
.B \-\-dedup\-key
skips a repeated notification, and with 2 when the deduplication state
cannot be used.
.B \-\-preview
exits with 1 when rendering or writing the output fails.
.PP
Errors are written to standard error in plain language, naming the failing step.
With
.BR \-\-json ,
the JSON document reports the same exit status in
.BR exit_code .
.SH EXAMPLES
Export and use a custom template:
.PP
.nf
mailfire export\-templates ./my\-templates
mailfire \-\-md \-\-template\-file ./my\-templates/card.html \e
  \-\-to user@example.com \-\-subject "Report" \-\-file report.md
.fi
.PP
Preview a custom template locally, without credentials:
.PP
.nf
mailfire \-\-md \-\-template\-file ./my\-templates/card.html \e
  \-\-footer footer.html \-\-file report.md \-\-preview report.html
.fi
.PP
Send a Markdown message from standard input:
.PP
.nf
mailfire \-\-md \-\-to user@example.com \-\-subject "Have a nice day" <<EOF
# This is an Email

It is written in markdown. It's delivered as HTML.
EOF
.fi
.PP
Send files as attachments:
.PP
.nf
mailfire \-\-to user@example.com \-\-subject "The files" \e
  \-\-attach ~/doc1.pdf \-\-attach ~/image.png
.fi
.PP
Verify configuration and payload without delivering anything:
.PP
.nf
mailfire \-\-test \-\-to user@example.com \-\-subject "Test" <<< "Hello"
.fi
.PP
Check configuration and credentials:
.PP
.nf
mailfire validate
.fi
.PP
List, then retry deferred messages:
.PP
.nf
mailfire resend \-\-list
mailfire resend
.fi
.PP
Alert at most once per 30 minutes, for example from a monitoring check:
.PP
.nf
mailfire \-\-dedup\-key disk\-full\-db1 \-\-cooldown 30m \e
  \-\-to ops@example.com \-\-subject "Disk full on db1" <<< "df: 98%"
.fi
.PP
Inspect and delete a deferred message:
.PP
.nf
mailfire queue stats
mailfire queue show 20261015T123045.123456789\-9f3ab2c17e4d88aa
mailfire queue delete 20261015T123045.123456789\-9f3ab2c17e4d88aa
.fi
.PP
Use the JSON output in scripts:
.PP
.nf
mailfire \-\-json \-\-to ops@example.com \-\-subject "Backup" \-\-message ok \e
  | jq \-r .message_id
mailfire queue list \-\-json \e
  | jq \-r '.messages[] | select(.status == "failed") | .id'
.fi
